<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jamgön Kongtrul</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jamgön Kongtrul Lodrö Thayé</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jamgön Kongtrul Lodrö Thayé was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> scholar and teacher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> and one of the founders of the non-sectarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rime-movement">Rimé movement</a>. Working with Jamyang Khyentse Wangpo, he gathered teachings from all the Tibetan traditions into vast collections known as the Five Treasuries. He was based at Palpung Monastery in the kingdom of Derge.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamgon-kongtrul.jpg" alt="Jamgön Kongtrul" width="240" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lodro_Thaye.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kongtrül was born in Rongyab in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a>, then part of the Derge Kingdom. He was first tonsured at a Bon monastery and at 20 became a monk at Shechen, a major Nyingma monastery of the region, before moving in 1833 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a> monastery of Palpung under the Ninth Tai Situ, Pema Nyinje Wangpo. There he studied Buddhist philosophy, tantra, medicine, architecture, poetics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, and by thirty he had received teachings and empowerments from more than sixty masters of the different schools.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He practised chiefly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a> and Nyingma traditions, including Mahamudra and Dzogchen, but also studied and taught Jonang Kalachakra, and he toured with the fourteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmapa">Karmapa</a>, whom he taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. He became influential in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in religion, administration and diplomacy, and helped save Palpung when an army of the Central Tibetan government occupied Kham in 1865. Amid the political and religious conflicts of his time he worked with Jamyang Khyentse Wangpo, the treasure revealer Chogyur Lingpa and Ju Mipham Gyatso to gather, exchange and revive the teachings of the Sakya, Kagyu and Nyingma, many of them near extinction.</p><p>His hermitage, Kunzang Dechen Osel Ling, the Garden of Auspicious Bliss and Clear Light, stood on a rocky outcrop above Palpung. It became an important centre for three-year retreats, and there he wrote most of his major works.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Kongtrül was also a poet, artist, physician and tertön, and composed over 90 volumes of Buddhist writing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khazneh">The Treasury</a> of Knowledge, ten books covering Buddhist history, philosophy and practice, was his central work and has been influential in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a> and Nyingma schools. The other treasuries gather empowerments and oral instructions, rituals, a vast compilation of termas, his own revelations, and writings on medicine and science. He held that all Buddhist traditions were worth study and preservation, and he promoted the shentong view of emptiness as the highest. The historian Sam van Schaik argues that without this collecting and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> of rare works, the later suppression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> by the Communists would have been far more final.</p><p>Several tulkus have been recognised as his incarnations, among them Karsé Kongtrül, a son of the 15th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmapa">Karmapa</a>, who died in 1952, and Karma Lodrö Chökyi Senge, born in 1954, who grew up at Rumtek Monastery in Sikkim and died in a road accident in Darjeeling District on 26 April 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jamgon Kongtrul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 19th century Derge patronised the non-sectarian Rime movement of teachers such as <b>Jamgön Kongtrul</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-derge">Kingdom of Derge</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dêgê, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.81,98.58&amp;year=1868" aria-label="Dêgê on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jamgön Kongtrul"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5621 4822h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5621 4822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.81,98.58&amp;year=1868">What was here in 1868? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
